Chapter: Tower
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter Num
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The radio tower beyond each intersection: a tall square-base lattice pyramid of
 metal rods tapering to an apex with a pink beacon. The owner places it; this
 chapter only renders.

 A port of wasm/tower.zig.

 THIS IS THE FLAT FORM ONLY -- the four faces drawn as a billboard lattice, which
 is what the TypeScript draws beyond its far distance. The zig diverges from the
 TypeScript in ONE way and the port keeps the divergence: every rod stays a 3D
 segment and is near-clipped BEFORE projecting, rather than interpolating points
 that are already on screen. The TypeScript could skip that because it only used
 the flat path for far towers, where nothing straddles the eye plane; here the
 flat path runs at all distances, so a tower you pass close would otherwise streak
 a rod clear across the viewport.

 The apex beacon BLINKS on the step clock, as a pure function of it, so it freezes
 on pause and runs backwards on reverse -- which is why the phase can be negative
 and why the modulo has to be the floored one.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

  tower-height : Real
  tower-height = 80.0

 Half the twelve-metre square base edge.

  tower-half : Real
  tower-half = 6.0

 A cross-beam ring every this many metres, and X-braces on the bottom two stages.

  stage-height : Real
  stage-height = 20.0

  brace-stages : Integer
  brace-stages = 2

  rod-half : Real
  rod-half = 0.12

  rod-w : Real
  rod-w = rod-half * 2.0

  tower-metal : Integer
  tower-metal = 10133672

 A local ground bulge, stronger than the road's, so a far tower sinks until its
 base meets the horizon.

  earth-radius : Real
  earth-radius = 20000.0

  beacon-radius : Real
  beacon-radius = 3.0

  beacon-color : Integer
  beacon-color = 16723942

 Frames for a full invisible-to-bright-to-invisible blink.

  beacon-period : Real
  beacon-period = 120.0

Section: The Beacon Clock

 Spread each tower's blink phase so they do not pulse in unison: 37 is coprime to
 the 120-frame period, so successive owners land on distinct offsets.

  beacon-offset-for : Integer -> Real
  beacon-offset-for (n) = real-from-int ((n * 37) - (((n * 37) / 120) * 120))

 Brightness is 0 at the cycle ends and 1 in the middle.

 THE DOUBLE MODULO IS THE ZIG'S AND IS KEPT. `@mod` is already non-negative for a
 positive divisor, so `@mod(@mod(p, P) + P, P)` is belt and braces -- but the port
 is a port, and a phase really can be negative here because the clock runs
 backwards on reverse.

  beacon-brightness : Real -> Real
  beacon-brightness (phase) =
    let wrapped = mod-real (mod-real phase beacon-period + beacon-period) beacon-period
    in (1.0 - r-cos (2.0 * pi * wrapped / beacon-period)) / 2.0

Section: The Square

 The four base corners in half-base units, before the yaw.

  corner-u : Integer -> Real
  corner-u (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 0.0 - 1.0

  corner-v : Integer -> Real
  corner-v (k) = if k == 0 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 1 then 0.0 - 1.0 else if k == 2 then 1.0 else 1.0

 Base corner k in the owning segment's bottom-left frame, given the tower's base
 centre and yaw. The caller maps it into the rider frame. At the apex the square
 shrinks to a point at the centre, so the apex needs no helper.

  base-corner-ax : Integer, Real, Real, Real -> AX
  base-corner-ax (k) (a0) (x0) (yaw) =
    let du = corner-u k * tower-half
    in let dv = corner-v k * tower-half
    in let cy = r-cos yaw
    in let sy = r-sin yaw
    in AX { a = a0 + (du * sy + dv * cy), x = x0 + (du * cy - dv * sy) }

Section: Geometry

  tower-ground-drop : RiderPt -> Real
  tower-ground-drop (p) = (p.right * p.right + p.forward * p.forward) / (2.0 * earth-radius)

  lerp3v : Vec3, Vec3, Real -> Vec3
  lerp3v (a) (b) (t) =
    Vec3 {
      right = a.right + (b.right - a.right) * t,
      forward = a.forward + (b.forward - a.forward) * t,
      height = a.height + (b.height - a.height) * t
    }

 Cross-section corner k at world height h, lowered by the ground drop. The square
 shrinks linearly toward the apex at the centre, so it is a base-to-centre lerp by
 h over the height. The corner stays a 3D point so each rod can be near-clipped
 before projecting.

  corner-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Integer, Real, Real -> Vec3
  corner-at (base) (center) (k) (h) (drop) =
    let t = h / tower-height
    in let bk = list-at base k
    in Vec3 { right = bk.right + (center.right - bk.right) * t, forward = bk.forward + (center.forward - bk.forward) * t, height = h - drop }

Section: Rods

 A thick screen-space line as a filled quad of width wpx.

  bar : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar (a) (b) (wpx) =
    let dx = b.x - a.x
    in let dy = b.y - a.y
    in let raw = real-sqrt (dx * dx + dy * dy)
    in let len = if raw < 0.0001 then 1.0 else raw
    in rod-quad a b ((0.0 - dy) / len * wpx / 2.0) (dx / len * wpx / 2.0)

 NAMED FOR ITS SECTION RATHER THAN ITS SHAPE, and the rename was forced by the
 bare-metal arm: `bar-quad` is also GuardRail's, and Codex chapters share one flat
 namespace. Each chapter sees its own definition so both compiled and ran
 correctly -- but a mention in a chapter that defines NEITHER resolves by the
 order the build globs files, which is not a property anything should rely on.
 The seed diagnoses it (CDX3006); the zig arm says nothing.

  rod-quad : ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rod-quad (a) (b) (ox) (oy) =
    push-poly tower-metal [ScreenPt { x = a.x + ox, y = a.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x + ox, y = b.y + oy }, ScreenPt { x = b.x - ox, y = b.y - oy }, ScreenPt { x = a.x - ox, y = a.y - oy }]

 One rod, near-clipped: a 3D segment clipped to the near plane before projecting,
 so a rod straddling the eye plane cannot fling a quad across the viewport. Both
 ends behind means skip; one behind means pull it to the plane.

  bar3d : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    let a-in = a.forward >= near
    in let b-in = b.forward >= near
    in if a-in then (if b-in then bar3d-draw a b wpx cf view-w else bar3d-draw a (bar3d-cut a b) wpx cf view-w)
       else (if b-in then bar3d-draw (bar3d-cut a b) b wpx cf view-w else [])

  bar3d-cut : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  bar3d-cut (a) (b) = lerp3v a b ((near - a.forward) / (b.forward - a.forward))

  bar3d-draw : Vec3, Vec3, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  bar3d-draw (a) (b) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    bar (project a cf view-w) (project b cf view-w) wpx

Section: The Tower

 Rod width in pixels at this depth, from two ground points one metre apart.

  rod-px : Real, Real, Real -> Real
  rod-px (forward) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in rod-w * (p1.x - p0.x)


  tower-legs : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Vec3, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  tower-legs (base) (center) (apex) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k clip-h drop) apex wpx cf view-w) & tower-legs base center apex clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

 Cross-beam rings at 20, 40 and 60, above the drop.

  ring-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  ring-at (base) (center) (h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else (bar3d (corner-at base center k h drop) (corner-at base center ((k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)) h drop) wpx cf view-w) & ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  rings : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  rings (base) (center) (h) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) =
    if h >= tower-height then []
    else (if h <= clip-h then [] else ring-at base center h drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rings base center (h + stage-height) clip-h drop wpx cf view-w

 X-braces on the bottom stages, each diagonal clipped at the drop height.

  brace-at : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-at (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else brace-pair base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w k

  brace-pair : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-pair (base) (center) (lo) (hi) (f) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (k) =
    let j = (k + 1) - (((k + 1) / 4) * 4)
    in let kj = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center k lo drop) (corner-at base center j hi drop) f) (corner-at base center j hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in let jk = bar3d (lerp3v (corner-at base center j lo drop) (corner-at base center k hi drop) f) (corner-at base center k hi drop) wpx cf view-w
    in kj & jk & brace-at base center lo hi f drop wpx cf view-w (k + 1)

  braces : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  braces (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    if stage >= brace-stages then []
    else brace-stage base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w stage

  brace-stage : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  brace-stage (base) (center) (clip-h) (drop) (wpx) (cf) (view-w) (stage) =
    let lo = real-from-int stage * stage-height
    in let hi = lo + stage-height
    in let rest = braces base center clip-h drop wpx cf view-w (stage + 1)
    in if hi <= clip-h then rest
       else (brace-at base center lo hi ((real-max lo clip-h - lo) / stage-height) drop wpx cf view-w 0) & rest

 The apex beacon: a pink disc whose SIZE scales with distance but whose BRIGHTNESS
 is the blink alpha -- a depthless glow composited over whatever is behind. Skips
 the invisible part of the cycle, and a sub-pixel disc.

  draw-beacon : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-beacon (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    if bright < 0.02 then []
    else beacon-disc apex-s forward cf view-w bright

  beacon-disc : ScreenPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  beacon-disc (apex-s) (forward) (cf) (view-w) (bright) =
    let p1 = project (Vec3 { right = 1.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let p0 = project (Vec3 { right = 0.0, forward = forward, height = 0.0 }) cf view-w
    in let r = beacon-radius * (p1.x - p0.x)
    in if r < 0.5 then [] else push-beacon beacon-color apex-s.x apex-s.y r bright

 Render a tower given its four base corners and base centre already mapped into
 the rider frame. Sinks it by the local ground drop; a tower sunk past its own
 height is below the horizon and is not drawn at all.

  draw-flat : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) =
    if center.forward < near then []
    else draw-flat-body base center cf view-w beacon-phase (tower-ground-drop center)

  draw-flat-body : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-body (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    if drop >= tower-height then []
    else draw-flat-rods base center cf view-w beacon-phase drop

  draw-flat-rods : List RiderPt, RiderPt, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  draw-flat-rods (base) (center) (cf) (view-w) (beacon-phase) (drop) =
    let apex = Vec3 { right = center.right, forward = center.forward, height = tower-height - drop }
    in let wpx = rod-px center.forward cf view-w
    in let l = tower-legs base center apex drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let r = rings base center stage-height drop drop wpx cf view-w
    in let x = braces base center drop drop wpx cf view-w 0
    in let b = draw-beacon (project apex cf view-w) center.forward cf view-w (beacon-brightness beacon-phase)
    in l & r & x & b
